<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1745 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">God Save the King</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of the United Kingdom, a short prayer for the monarch first printed in 1744. Nobody knows who wrote it.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (traditional; an attribution to John Bull has sometimes been made)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>British; later the British Empire and the Commonwealth realms</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>London, England (first published and first recorded performances)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first published 1744; sung in London theatres 1745</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics anonymous (first published 1744-1745))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The author of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; is unknown. The words of the title are much older than the song: a text built on 1 Kings 1:38–40 has been sung at every coronation since King Edgar's in 973, and by 1545 the phrase was a watchword of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. The music writer Percy Scholes found likenesses to an early plainsong melody, to galliard dance tunes, to a keyboard piece by John Bull (1619) and to pieces by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>, and recommended crediting the tune as &quot;traditional&quot;. Claims for Henry Carey and James Oswald he judged untenable or doubtful.</p><p>The first published version close to the present song appeared in 1744 under the heading &quot;For two voices&quot; in the anthology Thesaurus Musicus. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>, the song spread among supporters of King George II; it was sung at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> playhouses of Drury Lane and Covent Garden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-arne">Thomas Arne</a> wrote a setting for Drury Lane, and The Gentleman's Magazine printed it in October 1745. Tradition dates the first performance to after the government's defeat at Prestonpans. It is sometimes claimed that the words were first Jacobite, and drinking glasses of the early 18th century carry a version of them toasting James II and VII; Scholes thought both sides used the same words for their own kings. Verses were added and dropped for passing events: a prayer for Marshal Wade's army in 1745, a verse by Richard Sheridan after an attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>'s life in 1800, anti-French verses, and a republican version praising the guillotine by Joel Barlow in 1794.</p><p>The song became accepted as the British national anthem in the 1780s and 1790s. Its status rests on custom, not on any proclamation or act of Parliament. A more aggressive second verse was debated in the 19th century, and alternative versions were written by William Edward Hickson (1836), Samuel Reynolds Hole for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s Diamond Jubilee, and a &quot;peace version&quot; approved by the Privy Council in 1919; none replaced the standard words. An Army Order of 1933, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>'s preference, set its tempo and scoring. It was played at the BBC's close-down until 1997.</p><p>The song travelled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s national anthem until 1984, when it became the royal anthem; it remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s two national anthems and the royal anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, where a joint committee found in 1967 that its words and music were in the public domain. Rhodesia kept it after declaring independence in 1965, until it became a republic in 1970. Outside the empire the melody served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> (1795–1918), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> (1871–1918), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> (1816–1833) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> (until 1961), and it is still used for Liechtenstein's national anthem, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s royal anthem and the American song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a>&quot;.</p><p>About 140 composers have used the tune, among them Beethoven, who wrote piano variations on it and quoted it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>'s Victory. Haydn heard it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1794 and was moved to write an anthem for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperor. In 1951 it became the first music played on a computer and recorded, on the Manchester Mark II. Today usually only the first verse is sung; since 2003 the Commonwealth Games have not used it, and there are calls in the UK for a new anthem on secular, republican and English grounds.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer that God protect the reigning monarch and grant a long, victorious and happy reign. The often-sung second verse asks God to defeat the monarch's enemies, which is why it was debated and rewritten. The mood is solemn and loyal, close to a hymn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God save great George our King,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Long live our noble King,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God save the King!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Send him victorious.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Happy and glorious,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Long to reign over us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God save the King.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O Lord our God arise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Scatter his enemies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And make them fall.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Confound their politics,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Frustrate their knavish tricks!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On him our hearts are fix'd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O save us all.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thy choicest gifts in store,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On him be pleas'd to pour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Long may he reign!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May he defend our laws,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And ever give us cause,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To sing with heart and voice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God save the King.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O! grant him long to see</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Friendship and Unity,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Always increase.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May he his sceptre sway,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All loyal souls obey,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Join heart and voice, huzza!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God save the King!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;From ev'ry latent foe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;From the assassin's blow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;God save the King!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;O'er him thine arm extend,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;For Britain's sake defend</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Our Father, Prince, and Friend,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;God save the King!&quot;</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Anonymous; as printed in Burns' Celebrated Songs (Edinburgh, 1805). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Burns'_Celebrated_Songs_(1805%2C_Edinburgh)%2FGod_Save_the_King" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="X-0MndwZDGw" data-title="God Save the King: City of London Choir with the Royal Philharmonic Orchestra"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">City of London Choir with the Royal Philharmonic Orchestra</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel City of London Choir - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X-0MndwZDGw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1795</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (anthem of Prussia, later of the German Emperor)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Prayer of Russians</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1816</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (imperial anthem of the Russian Empire, 1816–1833)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rufst du, mein Vaterland</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (anthem of Switzerland until 1961)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Oben am jungen Rhein</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (national anthem of Liechtenstein)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kongesangen (Gud sign vår Konge god)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (royal anthem of Norway)</span></li><li><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dievs, svētī Kurzemi/Vidzemi!</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gott segne Sachsenland (Siegfried August Mahlmann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser (Haydn)</a><span class="sg-chain-h">inspired by (a different tune, written after Haydn heard this anthem in London in 1794)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Seven variations, WoO 78 (Beethoven)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1802–1803</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wellington's Victory (Beethoven)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 3 &quot;Great National Symphony&quot; (Clementi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1816–1824</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">model named by the composer</span></li><li><a href="/songs/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1874</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody reminiscent of it</span></li><li><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1880</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words may have been chosen for their likeness to it</span></li><li><a href="/songs/land-of-hope-and-glory">Land of Hope and Glory</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1902</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first five notes similar</span></li><li><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1888</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Siam's royal honour music before this anthem</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Coronation text on 1 Kings 1:38–40</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">sung since 973</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the title phrase</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Keyboard piece by John Bull</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1619</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible source of the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Remember O Thou Man (Scots carol)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">believed by 19th-century writers to be the source of the tune</span></li></ul></section>
